In <1990Aug7.200823.18256@wam.umd.edu> hawthorn@wam.umd.edu (Ian James Hawthorn) writes:

>When the <ctrl>y command is used, the cursor moves to the left and the 
>message `% Stopped' is printed. When processes are listed the process
>nethack has condition `T'.

This is because ^y is much the same as ^z (except that it waits for output
first). I'm using System V :-( so I don't see this problem, but I
remember similar problems with setuid programs and signals. For this
particular problem I would just use 'stty dsusp ^@' or whatever unused
character you want. dsusp is the ^y character (delayed suspend).

